    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6607B\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nthe matching award and tuition requirements of enhanced tuition awards\n\n PURPOSE OR IDEA OF GENERAL BILL :\n\nNew York State took further steps as a national leader in higher\neducation affordability with the launch of the Excelsior Scholarship\nand Enhanced Tuition Awards (ETA) in 2017. However, two years earlier,\nreacting to concerns about the escalating costs of higher education\nand the resultant impacts on student loan indebtedness, a private\nnot-for-profit institution - Utica College - became the first in the\nstate to substantial lower or \"reset\" its tuition. The new amount was\nset at $19,996, a reduction of approximately $14,000 per year or 42\npercent, a price point not seen since the 2003-04 academic year.\n\nIn the ETA program, there are two requirements for participation by a\ncollege: an agreement to match the State's $3,000 contribution to each\nstudent and a pledge to freeze the tuition of that student.\n\nIn the case of the matching requirement, Utica College used up the\nmeans to produce a $3,000 match due to the much-larger $14,000 annual\nreduction. By waiving the match requirement for institutions that\nlower their tuition by more than 15 percent, Utica and others would be\nable to participate in the ETA program, driving down the tuition cost\nfor New York State residents to $17,000 per year.\n\nIn the case of a tuition freeze for four years, it would take Utica\nCollege over 12 years to get back to the tuition level abandoned in\n2015 at annual increases of 3 percent, the amount of statutory\nincreases included for SUNY in the 2017-18 budget.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subsections 4, 5, and 6 of section 667-d\nof the education law to provide that if an institution lowered tuition\nby more than 15 percent over the last five years that such action\nshall be the equivalent of providing the matching award until 2026.\n\nIt also provides that if an institution lowered tuition by more than\n15 percent over the last five years that such action shall be the\nequivalent of providing the tuition freeze; provided that tuition is\nnot increased by more than 3 percent per year over the next nine\nyears.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date, which is immediate.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nReducing tuition through such substantial reductions or \"resets\" is\nclearly the type of behavior from private institutions that the State\nshould want to incentivize, but the ETA statute's match and freeze\nrequirements as written would unfortunately preclude them from\nparticipation, providing a disincentive for others to follow suit.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediate.\n",
